{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ישה", :translit "yashhe", :gloss "he will lend"} {:he "אשר ישה ברעהו", :translit "asher yashhe bere'ehu", :gloss "who lends his fellow"} {:he "כי ישה לך אלוה מעונך", :translit "ki yashhe lekha elohe me'onekha", :gloss "for God will lend you from your poverty"} {:he "שאם תשמט ישה לך אלוה מעונך", :translit "she'im tashmet yashhe lekha elohe me'onekha", :gloss "for if you release, God will lend you from your poverty"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: the repeated verb ישה links human lending with divine lending as reward for observing shemittat kesafim"}], :frame {:text "The Torah uses the verb ישה (to lend) twice in Deuteronomy 15:2 — once for human lending (אשר ישה ברעהו) and once for divine lending (כי ישה לך אלוה מעונך). Ba'al HaTurim reads this as a cause-and-effect promise: when one observes shemittat kesafim (the release of debts in the sabbatical year, Deuteronomy 15:1–2), God responds by lending to that person from divine 'poverty,' meaning He provides for them from hidden channels.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 15:2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: the repeated verb ישה links human lending with divine lending as reward for observing shemittat kesafim", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:36:43.501681013Z", :model "glm-5.2"}